# Day 008 – TMediaView Rendering Foundation

## Theme

Today's work moved MView from the basic first-image milestone toward a proper renderer and viewing-surface architecture.

The first image was successfully displayed, and we then implemented the first real rendering behavior: fitting the image to the available viewing area while preserving its original aspect ratio.

The guiding principle for today's work was:

> Build the rendering geometry cleanly now so zoom, pan, rotation, and future interaction can be added without redesigning the renderer.

## TMediaView and Renderer

The PaintBox was removed from the application.

TMediaView is now the application's drawing surface and fills the available client area of the form.

The renderer receives the TMediaView canvas and therefore works with the actual available viewing area rather than depending directly on the form dimensions.

Conceptually:

```
MainForm
    │
    ▼
TMediaView (drawing surface)
    │
    ▼
TRenderer.Paint(ACanvas)
```

This keeps the renderer independent of the surrounding form layout.

## Fit-to-Screen Rendering

The initial display behavior is now defined as:

- fit the complete image into the available viewing area
- preserve the original aspect ratio
- center the image
- show black background in unused areas

The fit calculation was separated into a function:

```
CalculateFit(ACanvasWidth, ACanvasHeight): Double
```

The function returns the scale required to fit the complete image into the available canvas.

Conceptually:

```
scaleX = viewport width  / image width
scaleY = viewport height / image height

FitScale = minimum(scaleX, scaleY)
```

The displayed dimensions are then calculated from FitScale, followed by the centered X/Y position.

## Canvas, Position and Size

An important distinction was clarified during implementation.

The canvas defines the available viewing area:

```
ACanvas.Width
ACanvas.Height
```

The bitmap already has its own width and height.

The Draw operation:

```
FDisplayBitmap.Draw(ACanvas, X, Y, True);
```

means:

- ACanvas = destination
- X/Y = top-left position of the bitmap on the destination canvas
- True = transparency handling

Width and height are not supplied to Draw because FDisplayBitmap has already been created at the desired display dimensions.

The scaling occurs earlier through Resample:

```
FBitmap
    │
    │ Resample(DisplayWidth, DisplayHeight)
    ▼
FDisplayBitmap
    │
    │ Draw(ACanvas, X, Y, True)
    ▼
TMediaView
```

## Display Bitmap Cache

We introduced a second bitmap in TRenderer:

```
FBitmap
FDisplayBitmap
```

FBitmap is the decoded source image.

FDisplayBitmap is the prepared image at the current display dimensions.

The renderer now resamples only when the required display dimensions change.

The logic is:

```
Paint
 │
 ├── calculate required display size
 │
 ├── size changed?
 │       │
 │       ├── YES → resample FBitmap
 │       │
 │       └── NO  → reuse FDisplayBitmap
 │
 └── draw FDisplayBitmap
```

This is an important performance improvement because ordinary repainting no longer causes a new resampling operation.

When a new image is loaded, the cached display dimensions are reset so the display bitmap will be regenerated for the new source image.

## TViewState

A view-state structure has been introduced in preparation for interaction:

```
TViewState = record
  Zoom: Double;
  PanX: Double;
  PanY: Double;
  Rotation: Integer;
end;
```

The state is initialized in TRenderer.Create.

At present the state is only partially used. It establishes the intended architecture for later zooming, panning and rotation.

## Agreed Viewing Behavior

We clarified the target behavior based on the original Hamana functionality.

### Initial display

For the current implementation:

- Fit-to-screen
- Preserve aspect ratio
- Center the image

A future interaction mode may allow the initial display behavior to depend on the mouse quadrant. One quadrant could select original-size display, while another could select fit-to-screen.

This is reserved for later and is not implemented now.

### Panning

Panning is intended to be unrestricted.

A large image displayed at original size should be freely movable through the viewing area.

The image may:

- be partially outside the viewport
- extend far beyond the viewport
- eventually be moved completely outside the viewport

Areas not covered by the image remain background.

When navigating to another image, the view should reset to a centered initial state.

### Zoom

The agreed initial zoom behavior is screen-centered zoom.

The conceptual relationship will be:

```
FitScale × Zoom = ActualScale
```

Therefore:

```
Zoom = 1.0  → fit-to-screen
Zoom = 2.0  → twice the fitted size
```

Zooming should preserve the screen center as the fixed point, even when the image is already partially off-screen.

A future enhancement is reserved for zooming around the mouse pointer. This is intentionally not part of the current implementation.

### Rotation

Rotation remains part of the planned renderer/view transformation and will be integrated after the basic zoom/pan behavior is established.

## Performance Considerations

The current display bitmap cache is a small renderer-level optimization.

It is not the future ImageCache described in Day 007.

The long-term architecture remains:

```
TMView
    │
    ├── TJobScheduler
    ├── TMediaLoader
    ├── TImageCache
    ├── TRenderer
    ├── TNavigator
    └── TDirectoryScanner
```

The renderer should eventually consume already-decoded images and concentrate on transformation and display.

Today's work reinforces the Day 007 principle that expensive operations should not happen unnecessarily during ordinary repainting.

## Current Milestone

The first image is displayed successfully.

The image now:

- fits the TMediaView surface
- preserves its aspect ratio
- is centered
- uses a black background for unused areas
- uses a cached display bitmap for repeated painting

The renderer has also been prepared for the future view-state model.

## Next Session

The next planned implementation step is:

1. Use FViewState.Zoom in the rendering calculation.
2. Establish:

```
FitScale × Zoom = ActualScale
```

3. Keep Zoom = 1.0 visually identical to the current fit-to-screen behavior.
4. Then introduce PanX/PanY.
5. Implement free panning before adding mouse interaction.
6. Later add screen-centered zoom interaction.
7. Keep cursor-centered zoom as a future enhancement.

## Key Insight

Today's work established an important separation:

```
Source bitmap
      ↓
Display-size bitmap
      ↓
Position on viewport
      ↓
Canvas
```

Scaling and preparation are separate from positioning.

This means that later panning can change only X/Y and does not need to resample the image. That is an important foundation for the fluid navigation behavior planned for MView.
